Exactly. **That is the direction I would push the paper.**

Right now, some parts of your method could be ported to another accelerator with relatively little conceptual change. For a really strong MLSys story, you want something where a reviewer can say:

> “This algorithm exists because of the execution model of Qualcomm Hexagon.”

Not merely:

> “They happened to run ZO on a Hexagon NPU.”

The strongest ideas are therefore things exploiting **Hexagon-specific constraints and primitives**: VTCM capacity, HMX/HVX execution, DMA/scratchpad behavior, quantized tensor layouts, dispatch granularity, synchronization costs, and the fact that you are feeding an inference-oriented runtime an optimization workload.

### The most interesting direction: make the optimization space itself Hexagon-native

Your current subspace idea is already close.

Instead of asking:

> “What low-rank subspace gives good optimization?”

ask:

> **“What parameterization can be represented inside the data movement and tensor formats that Hexagon already executes efficiently?”**

That leads to several much more hardware-specific possibilities.

### 1. VTCM-constrained perturbation staging

Your 3.2 GB crash is actually a huge clue.

Hexagon has a **small, explicitly managed local memory/scratchpad hierarchy**, so the natural question is:

$$
\text{How much perturbation state can actually coexist in VTCM?}
$$

Rather than merely saying “full rank is too large,” derive a **VTCM-aware maximum perturbation rank**:

$$
r_{\max}
=
f(B_{\text{VTCM}}, N, d, \text{dtype}, \text{number of slots})
$$

Then make the runtime choose

$$
r \le r_{\max}.
$$

Now rank isn't just an ML hyperparameter.

It becomes a **hardware resource allocation parameter**.

Even better, you could dynamically choose \(r\) based on the target layer's tensor dimensions and available VTCM.

That is extremely Hexagon-specific.

---

### 2. VTCM-resident coefficient cache + streamed basis

This is something I'd investigate seriously.

Your current perturbation is roughly:

$$
\Delta W = U_r Z V_r^\top.
$$

You don't necessarily want all of \(U_r,V_r,Z\) at every stage.

Could you keep the tiny \(Z\) / coefficient representation resident in VTCM and stream only the basis pieces needed for the HMX operation?

That turns the subspace representation into a **memory-hierarchy-aware representation**.

Then you can measure:

$$
\text{DRAM traffic}
\rightarrow
\text{VTCM traffic}
\rightarrow
\text{HMX compute}.
$$

That would be much more compelling than just "we fused kernels."

---

### 3. Exploit HMX's preferred matrix shapes

This is the direction I'd be most excited about.

Don't choose \(r\) solely based on optimization quality.

Choose \(r\) based on **Hexagon HMX tile/packing constraints**.

Suppose the hardware has preferred matrix dimensions. Then ranks such as 64, 128, 256 may map substantially better than arbitrary ranks.

You could formulate:

$$
r^*
=
\arg\min_r
\frac{T_{\text{HMX}}(r)+T_{\text{dispatch}}(r)}
{\text{optimization progress}(r)}.
$$

Then your rank isn't merely:

> low rank = less computation.

It becomes:

> **rank is quantized by the accelerator's preferred execution shapes.**

That is unmistakably hardware/software co-design.

---

### 4. Hexagon-specific dispatch amortization

You already have the beginnings of this with the \(r=8\) vs \(r=256\) crossover.

Push it harder.

Measure:

$$
T(r,N)=T_{\text{dispatch}}(N)+T_{\text{compute}}(r,N)
$$

and discover the point where HMX becomes worthwhile.

Then have the runtime choose:

$$
\text{CPU if }T_{\text{CPU}} < T_{\text{NPU}},
$$

otherwise NPU.

That's a **heterogeneous optimizer runtime**, not just an NPU implementation.

The really nice experiment would be:

> same training algorithm, same model, same rank, but automatically switching CPU/NPU based on rank and current workload.

Then the system itself learns the hardware operating point.

That could become one of the paper's strongest contributions.

---

### 5. NPU-native subspace refresh exploiting batched HMX

Your current block-power iteration is already excellent for this.

But I would frame it even more strongly:

The algorithm naturally wants repeated matrix-vector operations:

$$
Wv_i
$$

but Hexagon wants matrix-matrix operations.

So you transform

$$
W[v_1,\ldots,v_r]
$$

into one batched matrix multiplication.

That is a classic **algorithm-to-hardware transformation**.

And unlike activation-guided PCA, this is deeply tied to the hardware execution model.

You could potentially generalize this:

> **Any operation in the ZO optimizer whose mathematical form is GEMV is batch-transformed into GEMM to match HMX.**

That gives you a reusable systems principle.

---

### 6. Quantization-scale mutation — but make it Hexagon-specific

This is where your second idea becomes really interesting.

Don't pitch it as:

> "We optimize quantization scales."

Pitch it as:

> **"We exploit the fact that Hexagon's quantized execution path already consumes scale metadata, allowing optimization to modify metadata rather than the packed weight payload."**

Now the contribution is fundamentally about the **representation consumed by the NPU**.

Your packed 4-bit codes remain immutable:

$$
Q_b = \text{constant}.
$$

Only:

$$
s_b
$$

moves.

That potentially means:

$$
\text{huge weight tensor}
\quad\text{stays resident}
$$

while only tiny metadata moves through the update path.

If the actual Qualcomm kernel path lets you do this without invalidating packing/caching assumptions, **this is much more Hexagon-specific than generic low-rank ZO.**

And critically, you can compare:

| Approach                | Weight traffic | VTCM footprint |  NPU kernel changes | Trainable DOF |
| ----------------------- | -------------: | -------------: | ------------------: | ------------: |
| Full real-weight ZO     |           huge |           huge |               major |          huge |
| Low-rank real-weight ZO |       moderate |          small |            moderate |       \(r^2\) |
| Scale-only ZO           |           tiny |           tiny | potentially minimal | #scale blocks |

That would make a fantastic systems figure.

