## Something even more Hexagon-specific: exploit the runtime's quantized dequantization path

I'd investigate whether you can construct the perturbation **before or inside the dequantization stage**, rather than producing a separate perturbed weight tensor.

Conceptually:

$$
W = sQ
$$

and perturb:

$$
W'=(s+\delta s)Q.
$$

If the kernel already does something equivalent to

$$
Q \rightarrow \text{scale} \rightarrow \text{HMX multiply},
$$

then your optimizer can modify the scalar metadata feeding that path.

That is much more interesting than adding another standalone optimizer kernel.

The ideal systems story becomes:

> **We do not modify the inference engine to support training; we reinterpret existing quantized inference metadata as the trainable state.**

That is *very* MLSys.

---

# And there is another Hexagon-specific axis I'd explore

### DMA/transfer overlap

A mobile NPU isn't just compute.

You have:

**host memory ↔ VTCM ↔ accelerator compute.**

Could you overlap:

$$
\text{next perturbation transfer}
$$

with

$$
\text{current HMX computation}?
$$

For a ZO workload with many directions, this is naturally pipelinable.

For example:

$$
\boxed{\text{DMA}_i}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{HMX}_i}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{DMA}_{i+1}}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{HMX}_{i+1}}
$$

rather than:

$$
\text{DMA}_i \rightarrow \text{HMX}_i \rightarrow \text{DMA}_{i+1}.
$$

If you can demonstrate meaningful overlap on the real Hexagon stack, **that is pure systems contribution** and extremely difficult to dismiss as generic ML optimization.

